### Page 6 of 17

```text
UDC-22-00425-02
January 3, 2023
2)
The application reflects sound planning and urban design principles with respect to existing
and potential future development in the vicinity, including consideration of current and future
streets, open spaces, blocks and lots, and public utilities or facilities. (UDC 2.1.2.d.2)

The proposed application combines properties to create one larger lot. Immediately to the north
and east are streets; to the west, the land is already developed with no connections to this
parcel; and to the south, the land is currently vacant, but that property has access to all
infrastructure to the south.

The application complies with this review criterion.

3) The division of land meets the purposes, intent, and all applicable standards in Article 4. (UDC
2.1.2.d.3)

Community Facilities and Fees (4.2):
The proposed subdivision is small and in an area with existing facilities. It appears no public
facilities (such as a fire station or public park) are needed with this plat. For fees based on
acreage, the area of the plat is pro-rated to the nearest tenth of an acre. Fees based on 10.7
acres of land:
Land Acquisition Fee

$3,745 ($350 per acre)
Infrastructure Fee

$3,745 ($350 per acre)
Total Land Acquisition & Infrastructure Fees
$7,490.00
Per UDC Section 4.2.5, Table 4-2, the Land Acquisition & Infrastructure fee are payable at
the time of the signing of the final plat.
Per UDC Section 4.2.5, Table 4-2, Enhancement Fees are payable at the issuance of a
building permit for each structure (Residential Enhancement Fee is $400 per dwelling unit).

Transportation Network (4.3):
This plat is occurring within an area of the community which has largely been previously
developed. The plat could add a street connection with a stub out to the south. With the
plat proposing multifamily development and the property to the south having all needed
infrastructure to the south of it, a road is not necessary for future development. An
exception for block sizes must be granted. While the existing block is about 180% oversize,
any north-south division of this block would still likely result in at least one oversize block,
and possibly in double-fronted lots, which would require a separate exception to this
section of code.

Open Space (4.4):
Open space is required at 750sqft per unit or 8% of the gross development area, whichever
is less. However, due to the nature of this application, plat-level open space is not required.

Required Improvements (4.5):
The street adjacent to the plat to the north is substandard and should be developed with
this development. Utilities appear to exist in the area, although some extensions may be
needed to service the development.

Public Safety Fees (4.6):
Public Safety Fees will be due with building permits of new construction on the site.

The application complies with this review criterion, with an exception.
```

